WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:06.450
Hello. I am David Kaplan and I am not an expert.
But I hope in this series to learn as much

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:09.550
as possible and explain it to you the best
way I can.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:25.339
What happens when you fall into a black hole? We do not know. If you believe
Einstein's theory of relativity, we do know.

00:00:25.339 --> 00:00:30.539
The person falling into the black hole crosses
the horizon of the black hole. And if the

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:35.059
black hole is big enough, the person doesn't
notice. They just keep on going. And if they

00:00:35.059 --> 00:00:39.829
get close enough to the singularity at the
center of the black hole, bad things happen

00:00:39.829 --> 00:00:45.870
to their body. They get stretched out, warped,
and gravity is so strong that it's very bad.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:52.699
So we do have a description in that theory.
On the other hand, in quantum mechanics, if

00:00:52.699 --> 00:00:58.320
you have a situation where there's a horizon
— a surface where nothing can come out from

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:05.070
behind it — then quantum fluctuations at
that horizon produce radiation and the radiation

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:10.420
comes out. And you could measure that, in
principle. But if that energy is coming out

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:15.320
of the black hole, even from just the horizon,
the black hole should shrink and eventually

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:21.210
disappear. And whatever had fallen into the
black hole essentially disappears. And the

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:26.630
confusing thing is that quantum mechanics
doesn't predict the disappearance of any information.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:31.310
And so we would like to understand how the
information that went into the black hole

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:36.920
comes back out. And that requires describing
the inside of a black hole. But that means

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:41.540
that the theory of general relativity, which
predicts nothing interesting happening at

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:47.340
the horizon, smells like it's in contraction
with the theory of quantum mechanics, which

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:51.730
suggests that the horizon is a very important
place. So there's no complete description

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:55.980
of the interior of the black hole, which means
we don't know what happens. We don't really

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:01.260
care about what happens to somebody who falls
in. What we care about is attempting to describe

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:06.700
quantum mechanics and general relativity in
the same theory, and a black hole is the perfect

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:08.530
place because it requires both.

